            <ArticleTitle>Non-invasive and invasive imaging of vulnerable coronary plaque.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>538-47</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.tcm.2016.03.005</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1050-1738(16)00081-5</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Vulnerable plaque is characterized by a large necrotic core and an overlying thin fibrous cap. Non-invasive imaging modalities such as computed tomography angiography (CTA) and magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) allow for the assessment of morphological plaque characteristics, while positron emission tomography (PET) enables the detection of metabolic activity within the atherosclerotic lesions. Invasive imaging modalities such as intravascular ultrasound (IVUS), optical-coherence tomography (OCT), and intravascular MRI (IV-MRI) display plaques at a high spatial resolution. Near-infrared spectroscopy (NIRS) allows for the detection of chemical components of atherosclerotic plaques. In this review, we describe state-of-the-art non-invasive and invasive imaging modalities and stress the combination of their advantages to identify vulnerable plaque features.</AbstractText>
